CAT = "phrebAppraisal"; SEG = "APR"
Q = [
("Valuing a house by comparing it with recent sales of similar houses nearby, adjusted for differences, is the:",
 "Sales comparison approach", ["Cost approach", "Income approach", "Residual approach"],
 "The sales comparison (market) approach adjusts the prices of comparable sales for differences in location, size, condition and date to estimate the subject's value."),
("The approach best suited to valuing a new public school building with no comparable sales or rental income is the:",
 "Cost approach", ["Income approach", "Sales comparison approach", "Gross rent multiplier"],
 "Special-purpose properties rarely sell or rent, so their value is estimated as land value plus the depreciated cost of reproducing or replacing the improvements."),
("An appraiser estimates what it would cost today to build an exact replica of a heritage house, with the same materials and design. This is its:",
 "Reproduction cost", ["Replacement cost", "Assessed value", "Salvage value"],
 "Reproduction cost is the cost of an exact duplicate. Replacement cost is the cost of a building with the same utility using current materials and design."),
("Value is greatest when land, labor, capital and management are combined in the right proportion. This is the principle of:",
 "Balance", ["Substitution", "Progression", "Anticipation"],
 "The principle of balance holds that over- or under-investing in any agent of production, such as too much building for the land, lowers the property's value."),
("The period over which a building is expected to add value to the property, usually shorter than its physical life, is its:",
 "Economic life", ["Physical life", "Chronological age", "Loan term"],
 "Economic life ends when the improvement no longer contributes value over the land alone, often well before the structure physically wears out."),
("A house near the sea in Cebu has roof sheets badly rusted by salt air that must be replaced. This loss in value is:",
 "Physical deterioration", ["Functional obsolescence", "External obsolescence", "Economic appreciation"],
 "Physical deterioration is wear and tear from use, age and the elements. Rusted roofing that is economical to replace is usually curable."),
("A residence loses value because a landfill opened next door. This loss is:",
 "External obsolescence", ["Functional obsolescence", "Physical deterioration", "Curable depreciation"],
 "External (economic) obsolescence arises from factors outside the property, such as nuisances, traffic or market decline, and is usually incurable by the owner."),
("A building costs 9,600,000 pesos new and has a 40-year economic life. Using straight-line depreciation, its depreciated cost after 15 years is:",
 "6,000,000 pesos", ["3,600,000 pesos", "6,240,000 pesos", "9,360,000 pesos"],
 "Yearly depreciation = 9,600,000 / 40 = 240,000. After 15 years: 15 x 240,000 = 3,600,000, so the depreciated cost is 9,600,000 - 3,600,000 = 6,000,000 pesos."),
("An apartment earns a net operating income of 840,000 pesos a year, and the market capitalization rate is 7%. Its estimated value is:",
 "12,000,000 pesos", ["5,880,000 pesos", "8,400,000 pesos", "14,700,000 pesos"],
 "Direct capitalization: value = income / rate = 840,000 / 0.07 = 12,000,000 pesos."),
("A warehouse sold for 25,000,000 pesos and earns a net operating income of 2,250,000 pesos a year. The capitalization rate is:",
 "9%", ["8%", "11.1%", "12%"],
 "Cap rate = net operating income / price = 2,250,000 / 25,000,000 = 0.09, or 9%."),
("A row of townhouses rents for 42,000 pesos a month each, and similar units sell at a gross monthly rent multiplier of 150. One unit's value is about:",
 "6,300,000 pesos", ["4,200,000 pesos", "5,040,000 pesos", "7,560,000 pesos"],
 "Value = monthly rent x GRM = 42,000 x 150 = 6,300,000 pesos."),
("The final step in which the appraiser weighs the results of the different approaches into one value opinion is:",
 "Reconciliation", ["Capitalization", "Depreciation", "Abstraction"],
 "In reconciliation the appraiser judges the reliability of each approach for the property type and data available, then settles on a single value."),
("A comparable lot sold for 18,000 pesos per sq m but has a corner location worth 10% more than the subject. The adjusted price for the subject is:",
 "16,200 pesos per sq m", ["16,364 pesos per sq m", "18,000 pesos per sq m", "19,800 pesos per sq m"],
 "Adjust the comparable toward the subject: the comparable is better, so subtract 10%: 18,000 x 0.90 = 16,200 pesos per square meter."),
("The values the BIR sets by location for computing taxes on real property transfers are called:",
 "Zonal values", ["Assessed values", "Book values", "Insured values"],
 "Section 6(E) of the Tax Code has the BIR set zonal values. For internal revenue taxes the higher of the zonal value and the assessor's value is used, and capital gains tax is on the higher of that and the selling price."),
("The tax base for real property taxes and, under R.A. 12001, a reference for national taxes too, is the local:",
 "Schedule of market values", ["Bank appraisal report", "Owner's asking price", "Broker's price opinion"],
 "Under R.A. 12001 the SMV, prepared by assessors and approved under BLGF review, is used for real property taxation and as a basis for other property-related taxes."),
("Market value is the estimated amount for which a property would exchange between a willing buyer and a willing seller:",
 "At arm's length after proper marketing", ["In a forced foreclosure sale ordered by a court", "Between close relatives", "At the original cost"],
 "Under the International and Philippine Valuation Standards, market value assumes an arm's-length transaction after proper marketing, with parties acting knowledgeably, prudently and without compulsion."),
]
